    "body": "# AI assisted\n\nThe middle policy. The author writes the text; a model may work on what the author has\nalready written, but may not produce prose of its own.\n\nAdopt this for a book whose author wants machine help with revision, but whose sentences\nmust originate with a person.\n\n## May a model write text that lands in `manuscript/`?\n\nOnly as a change to text the author wrote. A model may tighten, correct, re-punctuate and\nreorder the author's sentences. It may not supply a sentence that did not exist, fill a\ngap, continue a scene or draft a passage for the author to react to.\n\nThe test is origin, not amount: who produced the words in the first place.\n\n## What may a model do to the manuscript instead?\n\nEverything reporting-only that `human-authored` permits, plus the applied edits above.\n\n## What may a model do outside `manuscript/`?\n\nAnything. Support documents, tooling, configuration and publication assembly are\nunrestricted, and need no labelling.\n\n## Where findings go\n\nApplied directly when they are corrections to existing text. Anything that would add\nprose goes to `outline/open-questions.md` instead, because adding prose is the act this\npolicy reserves to the author.\n\n## What the author must be told\n\nAny edit that changes meaning rather than mechanics. A misplaced comma is mechanics; a\ncut clause is meaning.\n\n## Grounding\n\nAmazon KDP draws exactly this line, and this policy's name is its term.\n\n> \"If you created the content yourself, and used AI-based tools to edit, refine,\n> error-check, or otherwise improve that content … then it is considered 'AI-assisted'\n> and not 'AI-generated.'\"\n\n— Amazon KDP, *Content Guidelines*,\n<https://kdp.amazon.com/en_US/help/topic/G200672390> (no revision date stated)\n\nKDP requires no disclosure for AI-assisted content.\n\n**Hachette Book Group** publishes the clearest author-facing list of any large house, and\nit describes this tier almost exactly. Its pre-approved uses include \"Brainstorming book\nor character ideas\", \"Researching elements of a book\" and:\n\n> \"Editing, correcting or otherwise refining text or other content.\"\n\nAgainst which it sets the boundary:\n\n> \"Use of AI to generate content based on text prompts is not permitted.\"\n\n— Hachette Book Group, *AI Technology and HBG: Guidance for HBG Authors*,\n<https://www.hbgauthorresources.com/landing-page/info-for-authors-home/info-for-authors-author-ai-faq/>\n(last updated 20 March 2025)\n\nHachette frames the same split as **operational** versus **creative** use — the latter\nbeing any use that \"harnesses that technology to replace the creative work of a human\nauthor\". That is the line this policy draws: refining is operational, originating is\ncreative.\n\n**A caution.** This tier is looser than the Authors Guild's Human Authored mark, and in a\nway that is easy to miss. The Guild caps at de minimis the text \"generated **or\nmodified** by or with the use of GAI\" — modification counts, not only generation. Heavy\nmachine rewriting of human-written sentences satisfies KDP and Hachette here while\nquietly breaking the Guild's criteria. The Guild says of a full machine edit only that it\n\"may or may not result in a de minimis or less amount of AI generated text\", and advises\nasking a copyright attorney. A book under this policy should not claim the Human Authored\nmark without checking the actual work against the Guild's criteria.",
