    "body": "# AI generated\n\nThe permissive policy. A model may produce manuscript prose, working to the author's\ndirection, canon and voice rules.\n\nAdopt this only for a book whose author has said so. It is the default for none of them.\n\n## May a model write text that lands in `manuscript/`?\n\nYes — drafting, continuing, rewriting and expanding, within the book's established\nconstraints. The author remains the work's author in every sense that matters: they\ndirect it, revise it, decide what stays, and own it.\n\n## What may a model do to the manuscript instead?\n\nNot applicable; writing is permitted. The usual craft constraints still bind — a book's\nstyle guide, canon, timeline and voice rules outrank a model's instincts, and a clean\nlint run is never a reason to change a sentence the author settled.\n\n## What may a model do outside `manuscript/`?\n\nAnything.\n\n## Where findings go\n\nApplied, or raised in `outline/open-questions.md` when they turn on a decision the author\nhas not made.\n\n## What the author must be told\n\nNew canon. A model may write prose under this policy; it may not quietly decide a fact,\na date, a name or an outcome that the book's support documents do not already establish.\n\n## Disclosure\n\nText drafted by a model counts as **AI-generated** under Amazon KDP's definition even\nafter heavy revision, and must be declared at upload. That obligation belongs to the\nbook's publication step, not to this repository — but it follows from this policy, and\nwhoever prepares the edition needs to know it.\n\n## Grounding\n\n> \"We define AI-generated content as text, images, or translations created by an AI-based\n> tool … it is considered 'AI-generated,' even if you applied substantial edits\n> afterwards.\"\n\n— Amazon KDP, *Content Guidelines*,\n<https://kdp.amazon.com/en_US/help/topic/G200672390> (no revision date stated)\n\nKDP does not forbid AI-generated content; it requires it to be disclosed at upload, and\ntreats non-disclosure as a terms violation. A book under this policy cannot carry the\nAuthors Guild's Human Authored mark.\n\n**This tier closes doors.** Hachette Book Group, which publishes the clearest\nauthor-facing rules of any large house, forbids it outright:\n\n> \"Use of AI to generate content based on text prompts is not permitted.\"\n\n— Hachette Book Group, *AI Technology and HBG: Guidance for HBG Authors*,\n<https://www.hbgauthorresources.com/landing-page/info-for-authors-home/info-for-authors-author-ai-faq/>\n(last updated 20 March 2025)\n\nAdopt this policy knowing that. It is a legitimate way to make a book and Amazon will\ncarry it with a disclosure, but at least one major trade publisher will not accept the\nmanuscript, and the Authors Guild will not certify it.\n\nThe other large houses have published nothing this precise. Penguin Random House's public\nstatement — \"We do not see any technology as a substitute for human imagination\"\n(8 August 2024) — is an editorial stance and an anti-scraping position, not an\neligibility test for a manuscript, and is not cited here as one.",
