    "manuscriptWrites": "forbidden",
    "body": "# Human authored\n\nThe strictest policy. The manuscript is the author's work, sentence by sentence, and\nno machine contributes text to it.\n\nAdopt this for any book whose author has not agreed to machine-written prose — which\nincludes every book in this directory that is not Dominik's.\n\n## May a model write text that lands in `manuscript/`?\n\nNo. Not a sentence, not a phrase, not a single corrected character. This holds however\nobvious the fix, however explicit the request in the moment, and however much cleaner it\nwould make the lint output.\n\n## What may a model do to the manuscript instead?\n\nRead it, and report on it. It may run `check`, `lint`, `stats` and `build`; detect and\nlist misspellings, typography errors and structural problems; and propose changes for the\nauthor to accept or refuse.\n\n## What may a model do outside `manuscript/`?\n\nDraft support documents — `bible/`, `outline/`, `craft/`, `research/`, `README.md`,\n`CLAUDE.md` — provided they describe the book rather than invent it, and provided each\nsays so. *The Magic Mountain*'s style guide carries the form this takes:\n\"**Status: observed, not authored.** … It is not a mandate from the author.\"\n\nTooling, configuration, transcription and publication assembly are unrestricted, because\nnone of them originates prose.\n\n## Where findings go\n\n`outline/open-questions.md`, as questions for the author. A finding is not a task, and a\nlist of six uncorrected typos is the correct end state, not an unfinished one.\n\n## What the author must be told\n\nAnything ambiguous, before it is done. If a change cannot be made without deciding\nsomething on the author's behalf, it is not a change a model may make.\n\n## Grounding\n\nThe Authors Guild's **Human Authored** certification mark is the industry's strictest\npublished standard, and this policy's name comes from it.\n\n> \"the text itself was fully authored by one or more human beings and not generated by\n> AI, except for a de minimis amount … such as through the use of AI-powered spelling and\n> grammar check applications\"\n\n> \"a de minimis (e.g., very small or trifling) amount of text may be generated or\n> modified by or with the use of GAI\"\n\n> On whether AI brainstorming and outlining is allowed: \"Yes, as long as the AI use is\n> merely preparatory and no actual AI-generated text appears in your work.\"\n\n— Authors Guild, *Human Authored certification FAQ*,\n<https://authorsguild.org/human-authored/faq/> (no revision date stated; the page\ndescribes an updated mark as of March 2026)\n\nNote that the Guild's cap covers text **modified** by a model, not only text generated by\none: an aggressive machine edit of human sentences can break the mark as surely as a\nmachine-written paragraph. The Guild says a full machine edit \"may or may not result in a\nde minimis or less amount of AI generated text\", and points authors at a copyright\nattorney. A policy that permits no writes at all never has to make that judgement.\n\n**This policy is deliberately stricter than the mark it is named for.** The Guild allows\na de minimis amount of machine text — a spelling checker's corrections — to remain inside\na certified work. This policy allows none, because the boundary \"a model may write into\nthe manuscript, but only a little\" is not one an agent can be trusted to hold. Refusing\nevery write is a line that holds itself, and a book kept this way qualifies for the mark\nwith room to spare.",
